Line 102-104: `new Set(allEpisodes.map((ep) => (ep as Episode).season || 1))` — the Set generic is inferred as `number | undefined`? `(ep as Episode).season` is `number | undefined`, so `|| 1` gives number. Hmm, but TS says arithmetic error on a-b. Maybe Set inferred as `unknown`? If allEpisodes element type is a union where season could be weird... Actually if `(ep as Episode).season` is `any`? No — error TS2362 means type isn't number. Possibly Set<number | 1>... that's number.

Wait, maybe `.sort((a,b) => a - b)` — Array.from(new Set(...)) returns any[] if the map returns any. If episode.season were `any`, then no error. The error says must be of type any/number/bigint/enum — so it's something else like `string | undefined`. Perhaps `allEpisodes` inferred from union: selectedDetailMedia could be `MediaItem | null` narrowed; episodes typed Episode[]; seasons typed Season[]. flatMap result `{...Episode, season: number}`. Union fine.

Hmm — actually maybe the issue: `new Set()` without generic + downlevelIteration? No, error is arithmetic.

Simplest fix: type explicitly:
```ts
const availableSeasons = Array.from(
  new Set<number>(allEpisodes.map((ep) => (ep as Episode).season ?? 1))
).sort((a, b) => a - b);
```
If the mapped value somehow includes undefined due to exactOptionalPropertyTypes... `(ep as Episode).season || 1` can't be undefined. Unless `as Episode` cast failed because ep's type doesn't overlap? It compiled at line 94 though with same cast pattern... wait line 94 has same casts and didn't error — errors are only 103/19. So the Set inference produced something non-number. Explicit generic fixes it.

